GPT-6 (Codex)

1. HIGH — “No redundancy” is not established (review/draft6.md:85, 175, 1118, 1182, 1199). sweep_an.py:110–117 tests only the entire complement of one selected mask. Failure of that complement does not exclude a successful subset: ablation effects need not be monotone. Nor does it exclude overlapping redundant circuits. R_redundancy.exists=false means “this complement failed,” not “no disjoint DAG exists.” The reported 82–90% flips are correct; the inference is not.

2. HIGH — Necessity and impossibility claims exceed the measurements (draft6.md:1136, 1208–1216). No noisy width-16 model has sparsity results in summary.json; the 87–96% and 13–15 directions describe noiseless models only. Two failed noisy training runs do not establish that width 16 cannot tolerate sigma=0.01. Saliency-prefix sizes are sufficient constructions, not lower bounds on necessary structure, so they cannot rule out estimators for 10M-parameter models.

3. MEDIUM — The regression specification is incomplete and its interpretation too strong (draft6.md:1125–1130; summary.json keys L, d, sigma, M1, M4, M5). The stated +13 points/e-fold and +0.2 points/doubling reproduce only when a noise indicator is included; its omitted coefficient is −11.0 points. Using loss and width alone gives +10.5 and −3.8 points. The −19/−18 direction/single-edge coefficients likewise use the noise covariate. State the formula and all coefficients. These pooled associations do not establish behavior “at any loss”; training series, loss, width and noise coverage are confounded.

4. MEDIUM — “Matched loss” comparisons are asserted but not supplied (draft6.md:1149–1150; PREREG.md:339–341). The amendment requires each noisy model’s closest-loss noiseless comparison. sweep_table.py merely groups by series, width and sigma; section 10 reports same-seed twins and pooled regression. Those twins validly compare the training-noise intervention at fixed recipes, but do not hold achieved loss fixed. R9.2’s numerical twin clause passes; its matched-loss interpretation remains untested.

5. MEDIUM — Fractions support relative compression, not a common absolute complexity scale (draft6.md:83–87, 1107–1117, 1138). The denominator is 9d²+11d, mixing recurrent/input edges with bundled readout units; their proportions change with width. Reporting counts helps, but pooled fractions are not monotonically decreasing: L64 keeps 46–52%, versus S128’s 63–66%. “About 3x per doubling” also conceals L64→S128’s 4.8–5.7x count increase. Restrict ordering claims to comparable series.

6. MEDIUM — Measurement qualifications are missing (draft6.md:1151; sweep_an.py:68–85, 206–240). The “held-out” sample selects larger masks/ranks until they pass, as registered; it is therefore a validation sample, not independent confirmation of population flip ≤1%. Binary search assumes monotonicity and does not certify minimum size. M5 instead uses 8,192 fit inputs and a 0.1% threshold, so “all” measures use 32,768 held-out inputs is false. Flips concern correctness indicators, not predicted-label changes.

7. LOW / verification — Checked read-only at 2026-10-29 16:44 UTC: table ranges, counts, pass flags, twin differences, and saved M1–M4 flips agree with source JSON. Training compression, criterion, edge set and ablation implementations match Amendment 8. W8.2–W8.6 scoring is supported; W8.1 is supported only for retained runs under Amendment 10. “Random order cannot drop any edge” (1119–1120) is false: L_w64_s0_sig0.01.json B_random.K=37536 of 37568.
